Potential issues: NetInfo may initially report isConnected = false, causing disabled = true and button disabled. If the button is disabled, test may not be able to click it (since it only looks for clickable element?). The UI test may wait for button to become clickable (enabled). But perhaps NetInfo is not mocked properly, causing isOnline false, so button remains disabled, causing timeout.

Examining ExportScreen: they set isOnline state initially true. Then in useEffect, they add listener and also call NetInfo.fetch(). If NetInfo.fetch returns a promise that resolves with connectivity status; in tests, perhaps they mock NetInfo to default false or not defined, leaving isOnline false and button disabled.

But after testing the UI, they found button not found. If disabled, the button may still be present but not clickable; some test frameworks may still locate it. However the test error is "locator.click: Timeout 10000ms exceeded." So it found the element (maybe) but couldn't click. The logs show "visible text: FinanceMate ..." but not show the button text. So maybe the button is not visible because disabled? Or maybe the ExportScreen is not shown at all? But they did display "visible text" which includes some icons but not button names.

Potential reason: ExportScreen returns a view with two buttons: Export CSV, Export PDF. If they are using RNButton on web, then when running in phone simulation, Platform.OS may be 'android' or 'ios', so PrimaryButton is used. It renders a Pressable with style defined and Text child. The accessibilityLabel for the Pressable is set to `accessibilityLabel={title}` (title = 'Export CSV'), which should be fine. However the text of the button may not be recognized because it's inside Pressable -> Text. The test may look for a button element with accessible name, perhaps matched to accessibilityLabel or accessible name computed from Text content. The Pressable's accessible role is "button" via Pressable default? Pressable does not set accessibilityRole automatically; but in PrimaryButton component they set accessibilityRole="button". So should be fine.

So why didn't test find it? Perhaps the ExportScreen's renderButton is not called because isOnline may be false causing `disabled = loadingType !== null || !isOnline` => disabled = true => but they still render the button anyway. So button visible.

Alternatively, the test may target the "Export CSV" button by its visible text, not by accessibility label. For PrimaryButton, the Text inside has text content = title (Export CSV). That should be visible. Could the button be hidden behind some overlay? Not likely.

Maybe the issue is that ExportScreen not imported into navigation. Let's check App.tsx. They have Tab Navigator with ExportScreen component imported: `import ExportScreen from './src/screens/ExportScreen';`. So it's there.

But perhaps the ExportScreen screen not reachable as a separate screen via the Tab navigation because they also have a header which may overlay. However they show Export tab button and concurrently have a Header at top (SafeAreaView). That shouldn't block.

Could there be a missing "testID" or a missing accessible label causing the test to not locate it? The test used by UI flow likely uses a library (Playwright?) to interact with the UI. We need to see the UI test runner config. Let's search for UI test runner config.

